## The Problem: Object Reference Equality

When you pass an object as a prop, React uses **shallow comparison** (`===`) to check if props changed. Since objects are compared by reference, not value, a new object literal is always "different" even with identical contents.

```javascript
// This creates a NEW object reference on every render
function ParentComponent() {
  return <ChildComponent config={{ theme: "dark", size: 10 }} />;
  //                     ^^^ new object every render!
}
```

```javascript
// Even with memo, this still re-renders
const ChildComponent = React.memo(({ config }) => {
  return <div>{config.theme}</div>;
});
// memo's shallow comparison sees a new object reference → re-renders
```

---

## Solutions

### 1. Move the object outside the component (if it's static)
```javascript
// Defined once, same reference every time
const CONFIG = { theme: "dark", size: 10 };

function ParentComponent() {
  return <ChildComponent config={CONFIG} />;
}
```

### 2. Use `useMemo` (if it depends on state/props)
```javascript
function ParentComponent({ theme, size }) {
  const config = useMemo(() => ({
    theme,
    size
  }), [theme, size]); // only new reference when dependencies change

  return <ChildComponent config={config} />;
}
```

### 3. Use `useCallback` for function props (same issue applies)
```javascript
function ParentComponent() {
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log("clicked");
  }, []); // stable reference

  return <ChildComponent onClick={handleClick} />;
}
```

### 4. Pass primitive values instead of objects
```javascript
// Instead of passing the whole object...
<ChildComponent config={{ theme: "dark", size: 10 }} />

// Pass primitives directly
<ChildComponent theme="dark" size={10} />
// Now shallow comparison works correctly
```

---

## Quick Diagnosis Checklist

| Cause | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| Object/array literal in JSX | Move outside component or `useMemo` |
| Function defined inline | `useCallback` |
| Parent re-renders too often | Fix parent first |
| `React.memo` not applied | Wrap child in `memo` |

---

## Important Note

> Don't reach for `useMemo`/`useCallback` everywhere — only add them when you've **confirmed** unnecessary re-renders are causing a real performance problem. Premature optimization adds complexity without benefit.

Use the **React DevTools Profiler** to confirm which components are re-rendering and why before optimizing.
